WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.080
"No Time to Die" is all about endings, bringing&nbsp;
Daniel Craig's 15-year run as James Bond to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:11.040
close. It's a sprawling epic that's both&nbsp;
action-packed and tragic; it also does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:16.720
something no Bond movie before it has ever done.&nbsp;
This is the ending of "No Time to Die" explained.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:22.800
Every Bond movie has an extravagant villain with&nbsp;
an even more elaborate plan for world domination.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.520
"No Time to Die" has new threat Lyutsifer&nbsp;
Safin, a megalomaniac with a grudge and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:31.840
a peculiar worldview who thinks of&nbsp;
himself as a kindred spirit to Bond.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:37.680
"License to kill… I could be&nbsp;
speaking to my own reflection."

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:42.640
Earlier in the film, Bond traces a Russian&nbsp;
scientist to a SPECTRE meeting in Cuba.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:46.560
There, he discovers that Blofeld is&nbsp;
still running the shady organization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:49.600
from behind bars through&nbsp;
the use of his bionic eye.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:53.520
Blofeld attempts to use a bioweapon&nbsp;
that's been acquired from the scientist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.520
Orbuchev — a genetically targeted&nbsp;
virus made of nanobots called Project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:02.480
Heracles — to kill Bond. But Orbuchev,&nbsp;
at the behest of his true boss Safin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:08.080
reverses the programming of the weapon to kill&nbsp;
every member of SPECTRE in attendance instead.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:13.680
Safin saves Bond's life as he once did for&nbsp;
Madeleine Swann, Bond's estranged lover. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.720
Madeleine was a young girl, Safin sought revenge&nbsp;
against her father, Mr. White, for killing his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:24.640
family. He killed Madeleine's mother but chose to&nbsp;
spare her life, a mercy he uses for leverage later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:30.480
in the film to make her a pawn in his successful&nbsp;
plot to kill Blofeld and end SPECTRE forever.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:34.080
Bond has been suspicious of Madeleine&nbsp;
since the film's opening scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:39.440
when she led him into a trap at the grave of&nbsp;
Vesper Lynd. But Bond soon realizes the trap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:45.520
was entirely Blofeld's plan to drive them apart.&nbsp;
Seeing that she has been used by outside forces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:50.000
Bond runs to Norway to find Madeleine,&nbsp;
and finds a big surprise awaiting him.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:55.600
Five years pass in "No Time to Die" from the&nbsp;
film's harrowing opening breakup between Bond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.480
and Madeleine to the main thrust of the story.&nbsp;
But when Bond reunites with Madeleine and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:06.000
apologizes for the time they've lost while apart,&nbsp;
he discovers she has a daughter, Mathilde. Though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.800
Madeleine initially insists he is not the father,&nbsp;
it is very clear she is not telling the truth.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:14.880
After he meets his daughter, Madeleine&nbsp;
fills Bond in on her past with Safin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:18.320
how he saved her life and has&nbsp;
a strange obsession with her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:22.400
and how his parents used to be the&nbsp;
chief poison makers for SPECTRE.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.760
Bond, with help from Q, is able&nbsp;
to track Safin to his base on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:29.840
an island in murky international&nbsp;
waters between Russia and Japan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:34.960
where he is manufacturing Project Heracles on&nbsp;
a grand enough scale to deploy it globally.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:39.520
But before Bond can embark on his mission&nbsp;
to kill Safin and stop Project Heracles from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:44.080
spreading further, Safin and his men come&nbsp;
and kidnap Madeleine and their daughter.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.400
Bond must infiltrate Safin's island&nbsp;
with the help of the new 007, Nomi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:53.920
while trying to avoid sparking an international&nbsp;
incident and causing even more trouble for MI6,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:57.360
the agency that helped develop this&nbsp;
deadly weapon in the first place.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:01.520
Bond and Nomi storm Safin's&nbsp;
base, neutralizing Orbuchev&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:06.720
and mowing their way through Safin's henchmen.&nbsp;
Bond is able to reunite with the kidnapped pair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:10.640
and also he now knows for certain&nbsp;
that one is indeed his daughter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:15.600
Nomi gets them off the island to safety so he&nbsp;
can get to the center of the base and disable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:20.160
the blast door shielding that protects&nbsp;
Safin's operation from outside harm.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:25.520
M is prepared to authorize a surgical strike of&nbsp;
missiles to destroy Safin's island and prevent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:30.960
Project Heracles from being deployed, but it can't&nbsp;
be done until Bond destroys the defense system.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:36.400
It's a suitably harrowing, nail-biting sequence,&nbsp;
complete with a ticking clock of inbound missiles.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.800
But in his final altercation with Safin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:43.760
Bond is exposed to a version of the bioweapon&nbsp;
that's genetically locked to Madeleine's DNA.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:47.920
In killing Safin, Bond dooms himself,&nbsp;
because if he makes it off this island&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:52.640
and comes into contact with Madeleine or his&nbsp;
daughter, he will infect and kill them both.

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:56.400
Resigned to his fate, Bond lowers&nbsp;
the island's defenses and says his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:01.120
final goodbyes over comms to the love of his&nbsp;
life and the daughter he never got to know.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:04.960
James Bond is dead. Long live James Bond.

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:10.160
Back at MI-6 headquarters. M eulogizes&nbsp;
Bond by sharing a drink with Nomi, Q,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:15.200
Tanner, and Moneypenny. Over the course of the&nbsp;
last few films, each of these former colleagues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:19.680
has developed their own meaningful rapport with&nbsp;
Bond, and the knowledge that he died saving the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:24.480
world, cut off from a daughter he didn't even&nbsp;
know he had, weighs heavily on the entire group.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:29.040
Elsewhere, Madeleine drives off into&nbsp;
the sunset with daughter in tow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:34.080
still struggling with loss. But looking at&nbsp;
her daughter, who she remarks has James' eyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:39.680
she promises Mathilde to tell her a story. A&nbsp;
story about a man named Bond ... James Bond.

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:41.680
So what happens now?

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:43.120
"Do I look like I give a damn?"

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:45.680
James Bond has never died in a movie before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:49.760
despite 24 previous films full&nbsp;
of near-death experiences.

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:54.640
Because of the franchise's murky continuity,&nbsp;
none of the other Bonds' final outings were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:59.760
creatively conceived that way. Up until "Casino&nbsp;
Royale" intentionally rebooted the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:03.440
the first twenty movies essentially&nbsp;
followed the same James Bond.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:07.840
But with "No Time to Die" being imagined&nbsp;
from the outset as Craig's final outing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:13.520
the filmmakers felt free to tell a story with&nbsp;
far more finality than any Bond movie before it.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:18.080
But just because this Bond is dead doesn't&nbsp;
mean James Bond himself is being retired.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:22.880
The last message in the credits echoes what so&nbsp;
many other Bond movies before it have promised:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:28.240
"James Bond will return..." Not "007."&nbsp;
James Bond. That basically proves we'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:31.680
be getting another reboot of the&nbsp;
character in the next Bond movie.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:36.640
Leading up to "No Time to Die," new entries in&nbsp;
the Bond franchise have elicited think pieces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:40.160
about whether or not Bond can still&nbsp;
work onscreen in a changing world.

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:41.520
"Man talk."

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:48.000
"No Time to Die" proves you don't have to&nbsp;
drastically change Bond as a person — instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:52.720
you need to change the world around him&nbsp;
and create drama out of that juxtaposition.

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:56.080
Whether the next take on Bond is&nbsp;
a drastic departure or a careful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:59.760
refinement on what has come before,&nbsp;
the bold conclusion for "No Time to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:04.480
Die" has set the stage for Bond to&nbsp;
endure. Here's to the next 25 movies.

